{
  "title": "My day from morning to night",
  "title_target": "Мій день — Miy den",
  "goal": "Describe a whole day from waking up to going to bed, and master the group of -ся verbs that covers almost every daily routine.",
  "bekal": [
    "form verbs ending in -ся",
    "describe the order of your day",
    "say when your activities start and end",
    "use time connectors (спочатку, потім, після)",
    "say how often you do something"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Діалог — A student's ordinary day",
      "intro": "Oksana asks Ani to describe her day. Count how many verbs here end in **-ся**: almost everything you do to yourself belongs to that group.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Оксана:",
          "text": "[Ані|A-nee], [розкажи|roz-ka-ZHI] [про|pro] [свій|sveey] [день|den'].",
          "id": "Ani, tell me about your day.",
          "literal": "Ani, tell — about — one's-own — day."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Я|Ya] [прокидаюся|pro-ki-DA-yoo-sya] [о|o] [сьомій|SYO-meey].",
          "id": "I wake up at seven.",
          "literal": "I — wake-myself — at — the-seventh."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Оксана:",
          "text": "[Рано|RA-no]! [А|A] [потім|PO-teem]?",
          "id": "That's early! And then?",
          "literal": "early! and — then?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Вмиваюся|Vmi-VA-yoo-sya], [одягаюся|o-dya-HA-yoo-sya] [і|ee] [снідаю|SNEE-da-yoo].",
          "id": "I wash, get dressed, and have breakfast.",
          "literal": "wash-myself, dress-myself — and — breakfast."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Оксана:",
          "text": "[Що|Shcho] [ти|ti] [їси|yee-SI] [на|na] [сніданок|snee-DA-nok]?",
          "id": "What do you have for breakfast?",
          "literal": "what — you — eat — for — breakfast?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Зазвичай|Za-ZVI-chai] [кашу|KA-shoo] [і|ee] [каву|KA-voo]. [Потім|PO-teem] [іду|ee-DOO] [в|v] [університет|oo-nee-ver-si-TET].",
          "id": "Usually porridge and coffee. Then I go to the university.",
          "literal": "usually — porridge (accusative) — and — coffee. then — go — to — university."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Оксана:",
          "text": "[О|O] [котрій|kot-REEY] [починаються|po-chi-NA-yoot'-sya] [пари|PA-ri]?",
          "id": "What time do your classes start?",
          "literal": "at — which — begin — classes?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[О|O] [дев'ятій|deh-VYA-teey]. [Вчуся|VCHOO-sya] [до|do] [четвертої|chet-VER-to-yee].",
          "id": "At nine. I study until four.",
          "literal": "at — the-ninth. teach-myself — until — the-fourth (genitive)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Оксана:",
          "text": "[А|A] [ввечері|VVEH-cheh-ree]?",
          "id": "And in the evening?",
          "literal": "and — in-the-evening?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Повертаюся|Po-ver-TA-yoo-sya] [додому|do-DO-moo], [готую|ho-TOO-yoo] [вечерю|veh-CHEH-ryoo], [трохи|TRO-khi] [читаю|chi-TA-yoo].",
          "id": "I go back home, cook dinner, read a little.",
          "literal": "return-myself — homeward, cook — dinner, a-little — read."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Оксана:",
          "text": "[Коли|Ko-LI] [ти|ti] [лягаєш|lya-HA-yesh] [спати|SPA-ti]?",
          "id": "When do you go to bed?",
          "literal": "when — you — lie-down — to-sleep?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Близько|BLIZ'-ko] [одинадцятої|o-di-NAD-tsya-to-yee]. [Але|A-leh] [часто|CHAS-to] [пізніше|peez-NEE-sheh].",
          "id": "Around eleven. But often later.",
          "literal": "near — the-eleventh (genitive). but — often — later."
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**The ending -ся originally means \"oneself\", and that explanation is enough at A1.** *Мити* = to wash, *митися* = to wash oneself. *Одягати* = to dress (someone), *одягатися* = to get dressed. *Будити* = to wake (someone), *прокидатися* = to wake up. Once you see the pattern, dozens of verbs make sense at once.",
        "**The ending goes at the very end, after the person ending.** *Я прокидаю-**ся***, *ти прокидає-**шся***, *він прокидає-**ться***. Notice that the *ти* and *він* forms blend their sounds: written *-шся* and *-ється*, said *-sh-sya* and *-yeh-tsya*.",
        "**After a vowel, -ся often becomes -сь.** *Я вчуся* and *я вчусь* are both correct; the short form sounds more relaxed and is more common in conversation. Pick one and don't worry.",
        "**До + родовий (genitive) means \"until\".** *До четвертої* (until four), *до вечора* (until evening), *до понеділка* (until Monday). The same preposition also means \"to (a person or city)\": *до лікаря*, *до Києва*. Two meanings, one case.",
        "**Додому, not до дому.** It is written as one word and means \"home\" as a direction. Compare **вдома** (at home, a location). *Я йду додому* (I'm going home) versus *Я вдома* (I'm at home). ⚠️ Two very similar words that are very often mixed up.",
        "**Снідати, обідати, вечеряти: three verbs for three meals.** Ukrainian has its own verb for having breakfast, lunch, and dinner, much like English *to breakfast* or *to dine*. *Я снідаю о восьмій*: I have breakfast at eight. The nouns: *сніданок*, *обід*, *вечеря*.",
        "**Зазвичай = usually.** This word saves many sentences: it tells the listener you're describing a habit, not what happened today. Its relatives: *завжди* (always), *часто* (often), *іноді* (sometimes), *рідко* (rarely)."
      ],
